@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1748 = private unnamed_addr constant [1581 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary `list()`, `reversed()`, `set()`, `sorted()`, and\0A`tuple()` call within `list()`, `set()`, `sorted()`, and `tuple()` calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIt's unnecessary to double-cast or double-process iterables by wrapping\0Athe listed functions within an additional `list()`, `set()`, `sorted()`, or\0A`tuple()` call. Doing so is redundant and can be confusing for readers.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Alist(tuple(iterable))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Alist(iterable)\0A```\0A\0AThis rule applies to a variety of functions, including `list()`, `reversed()`,\0A`set()`, `sorted()`, and `tuple()`. For example:\0A\0A- Instead of `list(list(iterable))`, use `list(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `list(tuple(iterable))`, use `list(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `tuple(list(iterable))`, use `tuple(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `tuple(tuple(iterable))`, use `tuple(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `set(set(iterable))`, use `set(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `set(list(iterable))`, use `set(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `set(tuple(iterable))`, use `set(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `set(sorted(iterable))`, use `set(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `set(reversed(iterable))`, use `set(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `sorted(list(iterable))`, use `sorted(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `sorted(tuple(iterable))`, use `sorted(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `sorted(sorted(iterable))`, use `sorted(iterable)`.\0A- Instead of `sorted(reversed(iterable))`, use `sorted(iterable)`.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may occasionally drop comments\0Awhen rewriting the call. In most cases, though, comments will be preserved.\0A", align 1
