@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1766 = private unnamed_addr constant [1519 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for parameter annotations that contain redundant unions between\0Abuiltin numeric types (e.g., `int | float`).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe [typing specification] states:\0A\0A> Python\E2\80\99s numeric types `complex`, `float` and `int` are not subtypes of\0A> each other, but to support common use cases, the type system contains a\0A> straightforward shortcut: when an argument is annotated as having type\0A> `float`, an argument of type `int` is acceptable; similar, for an\0A> argument annotated as having type `complex`, arguments of type `float` or\0A> `int` are acceptable.\0A\0AAs such, a union that includes both `int` and `float` is redundant in the\0Aspecific context of a parameter annotation, as it is equivalent to a union\0Athat only includes `float`. For readability and clarity, unions should omit\0Aredundant elements.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```pyi\0Adef foo(x: float | int | str) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```pyi\0Adef foo(x: float | str) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as safe, unless the type annotation contains comments.\0A\0ANote that while the fix may flatten nested unions into a single top-level union,\0Athe semantics of the annotation will remain unchanged.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The numeric tower](https://docs.python.org/3/library/numbers.html#the-numeric-tower)\0A- [PEP 484: The numeric tower](https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#the-numeric-tower)\0A\0A[typing specification]: https://typing.python.org/en/latest/spec/special-types.html#special-cases-for-float-and-complex\0A", align 1
