@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.2071 = private unnamed_addr constant [1157 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for code that could be written more idiomatically using\0A[`str.removeprefix()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.removeprefix)\0Aor [`str.removesuffix()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.removesuffix).\0A\0ASpecifically, the rule flags code that conditionally removes a prefix or suffix\0Ausing a slice operation following an `if` test that uses `str.startswith()` or `str.endswith()`.\0A\0AThe rule is only applied if your project targets Python 3.9 or later.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe methods [`str.removeprefix()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.removeprefix)\0Aand [`str.removesuffix()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.removesuffix),\0Aintroduced in Python 3.9, have the same behavior while being more readable and efficient.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef example(filename: str, text: str):\0A    filename = filename[:-4] if filename.endswith(\22.txt\22) else filename\0A\0A    if text.startswith(\22pre\22):\0A        text = text[3:]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef example(filename: str, text: str):\0A    filename = filename.removesuffix(\22.txt\22)\0A    text = text.removeprefix(\22pre\22)\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.2072 = private unnamed_addr constant [68 x i8] c"Prefer `str.removeprefix()` over conditionally replacing with slice.", align 1
